"""Draft for Elective Compulsory Physics, Book 1, Chapters 3 (Mechanical Waves) and 4 (Light), People's Education Press 2019 edition."""
from phy_hs_lib import T, C, N

# ---------------- Chapter 3: Mechanical Waves
T("wave_formation", "Motion and Interactions", "conceptual",
  "A mechanical wave is the propagation of mechanical vibrations through a medium. Waves transfer the pattern of vibration and energy; particles in the medium vibrate around their equilibrium positions rather than traveling with the wave. "
  "Waves are classified as transverse or longitudinal according to the relationship between particle-vibration direction and wave-propagation direction. All particles begin vibrating in the same direction as the source.",
  ["Explain the conditions for forming a mechanical wave and that waves transfer vibration and energy, not particles",
   "Distinguish transverse from longitudinal waves and give examples",
   "Determine when particles in a medium begin vibrating and their initial direction"],
  [("S1.2.4", "exact")],
  pre=[("shm", "hard"), ("J:sound_source", "soft")],
  qs=[
    C("basic", "How do particles in a medium move as a mechanical wave travels through it?",
      "They vibrate around their own equilibrium positions and do not travel with the wave",
      ["They travel with the wave in its direction of propagation", "They move uniformly in the direction of propagation at the wave speed", "They always vibrate perpendicular to the direction of propagation"],
      "A wave transfers the pattern of vibration and energy. Particles vibrate around their equilibrium positions rather than traveling forward with the wave. Perpendicular vibration is characteristic of transverse waves; in longitudinal waves, particles vibrate parallel to the direction of propagation."),
    C("basic", "Which wave has particle vibrations perpendicular to its direction of propagation (a transverse wave)?",
      "A wave traveling along a horizontal rope, with the rope's particles moving up and down",
      ["Sound traveling through air", "A wave traveling along a spring, with alternating compressions and rarefactions", "A longitudinal seismic wave"],
      "In a transverse wave, particles vibrate perpendicular to the direction of propagation, as they do in a wave on a horizontal rope. Sound in air, compressions and rarefactions in a spring, and longitudinal seismic waves all involve particles vibrating back and forth along the direction of propagation."),
    C("standard", "The left end of a horizontal rope is the wave source. It initially moves upward, and the wave travels to the right. In which direction do the other particles on the rope initially move?",
      "Upward, in the same direction as the source's initial motion",
      ["Downward, opposite to the source's initial motion", "To the right along the rope, in the direction of wave propagation", "In different directions depending on each particle's position"],
      "Each particle begins vibrating when the preceding particle drives it. Its initial direction is the same as the source's, upward. The initial vibration direction is independent of the wave's direction of propagation and does not change with distance."),
    N("standard", "A wave travels to the right along a horizontal elastic rope at 4 m/s. Points A and B are 10 m apart, with B to the right of A. How many seconds after A does B begin to vibrate? (Enter a number only)",
      "2.5", "The wave travels at a constant speed, so the delay before B begins to vibrate is t = x/v = 10 m ÷ 4 m/s = 2.5 s. The particles themselves do not move at 4 m/s; that is the speed at which the vibration pattern propagates.",
      "10/4"),
  ])

T("wave_params", "Motion and Interactions", "procedural",
  "Waves are described by wavelength, frequency (or period), and wave speed: in one period, a wave travels one wavelength, and its speed is v = λ/T = λf. "
  "Frequency is determined by the source, while wave speed is determined by the medium. Thus, when a wave enters another medium, its frequency stays the same while its speed and wavelength change.",
  ["Define wavelength, frequency, period, and wave speed, and calculate with v = λf",
   "Know that the source determines wave frequency and the medium determines wave speed",
   "Determine how wave quantities change when a wave enters another medium"],
  [("S1.2.4", "exact")],
  pre=[("wave_formation", "hard"), ("shm_describe", "hard"), ("J:speed", "soft"), ("J:sound_feature", "soft")],
  math=[("cnm_p214", "At a constant wave speed, wavelength is inversely proportional to frequency; use inverse proportion to compare wavelength changes.")],
  qs=[
    C("basic", "A wave has a wavelength of 2 m and a frequency of 5 Hz. What is its speed?",
      "10 m/s", ["0.4 m/s", "2.5 m/s", "7 m/s"],
      "v = λf = 2 m × 5 Hz = 10 m/s. Using λ/f gives 0.4 m/s; using f/λ gives 2.5 m/s; adding λ and f gives 7, with inconsistent units.",
      verify="2*5"),
    C("basic", "During the time a wave source completes one full oscillation, how far does the wave travel, and how far does a medium particle travel if its amplitude is A?",
      "λ; 4A", ["λ; 0", "4A; λ", "2λ; 4A"],
      "In one period, the wave travels one wavelength λ. A particle completes one full oscillation and travels 4A (its displacement is 0). Particles do not travel with the wave, so wave travel distance and particle travel distance are different."),
    N("standard", "A source on the water completes 120 oscillations in 1 min. The distance between adjacent wave crests is 1.5 m. What is the wave speed in m/s? (Enter a number only)",
      "3", "The frequency is f = 120 ÷ 60 s = 2 Hz, so v = λf = 1.5 m × 2 Hz = 3 m/s. Using 120 directly as the frequency would give 180 m/s.",
      "1.5*120/60"),
    C("standard", "A source increases its vibration frequency while producing waves on the same rope. How do the wave speed and wavelength change?",
      "The wave speed stays the same, and the wavelength decreases", ["The wave speed increases, and the wavelength stays the same", "The wave speed and wavelength both increase", "The wave speed and wavelength both stay the same"],
      "Wave speed is determined by the medium, so it remains constant if the medium does not change. From λ = v/f, increasing frequency decreases wavelength. Assuming that higher frequency means higher wave speed confuses source vibration rate with wave propagation speed."),
  ])

T("wave_graph", "Motion and Interactions", "reasoning",
  "A wave graph shows each particle's displacement from equilibrium at one instant; a vibration graph shows one particle's displacement over time. "
  "Use a wave graph and propagation direction to determine particle-vibration direction (the slope method or same-side method), or use particle-vibration direction to determine wave-propagation direction.",
  ["Distinguish the axes and physical meaning of wave graphs and vibration graphs",
   "Use the slope method or same-side method to determine particle-vibration direction and wave-propagation direction",
   "Read wavelength and period from the two graphs and calculate wave speed"],
  [("S1.2.4", "exact")],
  pre=[("wave_params", "hard"), ("shm_describe", "hard"), ("shm", "soft")],
  math=[("cnm_h066", "A sinusoidal wave has a sine-curve shape; determining particle displacement and vibration direction uses the graph and properties of the sine function.")],
  qs=[
    C("basic", "Which statement correctly compares a wave graph with the vibration graph of one particle?",
      "A wave graph shows all particles' displacements at one instant; a vibration graph shows one particle's displacement over time",
      ["A wave graph shows one particle's displacement over time; a vibration graph shows all particles' displacements at one instant",
       "The horizontal axis of both graphs represents time",
       "The distance between adjacent crests on a wave graph represents the period"],
      "The horizontal axis of a wave graph is the particles' equilibrium position x; it shows each particle's displacement at one instant, and the distance between adjacent crests is the wavelength. A vibration graph uses time t on the horizontal axis and shows one particle's displacement over time; the time between adjacent crests is the period."),
    C("standard", "A sinusoidal transverse wave travels in the positive x direction and has the shape y = A sin(2πx/λ) at a certain instant. In which direction is particle P, whose equilibrium position is x = λ/8, vibrating?",
      "In the negative y direction", ["In the positive y direction", "In the positive x direction", "Its speed is zero; it is stationary"],
      "Looking in the direction of propagation, the wave at x = λ/8 slopes upward (displacement increases as x increases). By the slope method, the particle moves downward. A particle vibrates only along y, not x. P is not at a crest (the crest is at x = λ/4), so its speed is not zero."),
    N("basic", "A wave graph shows adjacent troughs at equilibrium positions x = 1 m and x = 5 m. A particle's vibration graph shows that it completes one full oscillation in 2 s starting at t = 0. What is the wave speed in m/s? (Enter a number only)",
      "2", "From the wave graph, λ = 5 m − 1 m = 4 m. From the vibration graph, T = 2 s, so v = λ/T = 4 m ÷ 2 s = 2 m/s. Read wavelength from the wave graph and period from the vibration graph; do not interchange them.",
      "(5-1)/2"),
    C("advanced", "At t = 0, a sinusoidal wave has the shape y = A sin(πx/2), where x is in meters and the wavelength is 4 m. The vibration graph of particle Q at x = 2 m shows a period of 0.4 s; at t = 0, Q is at equilibrium and moving in the positive y direction. What are the wave's direction of propagation and speed?",
      "In the positive x direction, 10 m/s",
      ["In the negative x direction, 10 m/s", "In the positive x direction, 2.5 m/s", "In the negative x direction, 2.5 m/s"],
      "At x = 2 m, the wave slopes downward when viewed toward +x (the displacement is negative at slightly larger x). If the wave travels in +x, a downward slope means the particle moves upward, consistent with Q, so the wave travels in the positive x direction. v = λ/T = 4 m ÷ 0.4 s = 10 m/s; 2.5 results from incorrectly using 1/T (frequency)."),
  ])

T("wave_boundary", "Motion and Interactions", "conceptual",
  "Waves reflect when they encounter an obstacle or boundary and refract when they enter another medium. Noticeable diffraction occurs when an obstacle or opening is about the same size as or smaller than the wavelength. "
  "During reflection, wave speed, frequency, and wavelength remain unchanged. During refraction, frequency stays the same while speed and wavelength change.",
  ["Explain wave reflection, refraction, and diffraction and the conditions for each",
   "Determine how wave speed, frequency, and wavelength change during reflection and refraction",
   "Perform simple calculations using wave reflection (echoes)"],
  [("S1.2.5", "exact")],
  pre=[("wave_params", "hard"), ("J:reflect", "soft"), ("J:refract", "soft")],
  qs=[
    C("basic", "A water wave with a wavelength of 2 m passes through one of the following slits. Through which slit is diffraction most noticeable?",
      "1.5 m", ["10 m", "30 m", "100 m"],
      "Diffraction is most noticeable when the slit width is about the same as or smaller than the wavelength. A 1.5 m slit is slightly narrower than the 2 m wavelength; as the slit becomes much wider, the wave travels more nearly in a straight line.",
      verify="1.5"),
    C("basic", "A water wave enters deeper water from shallow water and travels faster. Which statement is correct?",
      "Frequency stays the same and wavelength increases", ["Wavelength stays the same and frequency increases", "Frequency increases and wavelength stays the same", "Frequency decreases and wavelength increases"],
      "Frequency is determined by the source and does not change when a wave enters a new medium. From λ = v/f, an increase in wave speed means an increase in wavelength. Attributing the speed change to frequency is a common misconception."),
    C("standard", "People say, \"You can hear someone but not see them\" when voices can be heard around a corner but the speaker cannot be seen. What is the main reason?",
      "Sound wavelengths can be comparable to obstacle sizes and diffract, while light wavelengths are very small and diffract very little",
      ["Sound travels faster than light", "Walls reflect sound but not light", "Sound is longitudinal and light is transverse, so light cannot travel around a corner"],
      "Sound wavelengths range from a few tenths of a meter to several meters, comparable to doors and corners, so diffraction is noticeable. Visible-light wavelengths are only a few hundred nanometers, far smaller than obstacles, so diffraction is negligible and light travels nearly in a straight line. Sound is much slower than light, so speed is not the reason."),
    N("advanced", "A car travels toward a cliff at a constant 20 m/s. The driver honks and hears an echo 3 s later. If sound travels at 340 m/s, how far was the car from the cliff when it honked, in meters? (Enter a number only)",
      "540", "In 3 s, the distance traveled by the sound plus the distance traveled by the car equals twice the initial distance to the cliff: 2s = (340 + 20) × 3, so s = 540 m. Using s = 340 × 3 ÷ 2 = 510 m ignores the car's motion during that time.",
      "(340+20)*3/2"),
  ])

T("wave_interference", "Motion and Interactions", "reasoning",
  "When two waves with the same frequency and vibration direction meet with a constant phase difference, they form a stable interference pattern: vibrations are consistently reinforced in some regions and weakened in others. "
  "For in-phase sources, points with path difference Δr = nλ are reinforced, while points with Δr = (n + ½)λ are weakened. A reinforced point has maximum amplitude; its displacement is not always at a maximum.",
  ["State the conditions for stable interference",
   "Use the relationship between path difference and wavelength to identify reinforced and weakened points for in-phase (or out-of-phase) sources",
   "Correct misconceptions such as \"a reinforced point always has maximum displacement\""],
  [("S1.2.5", "exact")],
  pre=[("wave_params", "hard"), ("wave_boundary", "soft"), ("wave_graph", "soft")],
  qs=[
    C("basic", "What is required for two waves to produce stable interference?",
      "They have the same frequency and vibration direction, with a constant phase difference", ["They have the same amplitude", "They have the same speed", "They travel in opposite directions"],
      "Stable interference requires the same frequency (so the phase difference stays constant) and the same vibration direction. Equal amplitude or speed and opposite propagation directions are not required."),
    C("basic", "Two in-phase sources S₁ and S₂ emit waves with a wavelength of 2 m. Point P is 8 m from S₁ and 11 m from S₂. Which statement about P is correct?",
      "P is a point of destructive interference", ["P is a point of constructive interference with displacement always at its maximum", "P alternates between constructive and destructive interference", "P is a point of constructive interference with amplitude equal to the sum of the two wave amplitudes"],
      "The path difference is Δr = 11 m − 8 m = 3 m = 1.5λ = (n + ½)λ (n = 1), so P is a point of destructive interference. The positions of constructive and destructive points are determined by path difference and do not alternate over time; a constructive point still oscillates and its displacement is not always at a maximum."),
    N("standard", "Two in-phase sources S₁ and S₂ are 10 m apart and emit waves with a wavelength of 4 m. How many points between them on line S₁S₂ (excluding the sources) have constructive interference? (Enter a number only)",
      "5", "Let x be a point's distance from S₁. The path difference is Δr = |10 − 2x| = n·4 m, giving x = 5 − 2n (in meters). Since 0 < x < 10, n can be −2, −1, 0, 1, or 2, for five points: x = 9, 7, 5, 3, and 1 m.", "5"),
    C("advanced", "Two waves with the same frequency and vibration direction but opposite phases meet at point P. The difference in P's distances from the sources is Δr = 3λ. Which statement about P is correct?",
      "The vibration is weakened, with amplitude equal to the absolute difference between the two wave amplitudes",
      ["The vibration is reinforced, with amplitude equal to the sum of the two wave amplitudes", "It alternates between reinforcement and weakening, with amplitude changing over time", "It cannot be determined without knowing the wave speed"],
      "For out-of-phase sources, the conditions for constructive and destructive interference are reversed: points with Δr = nλ are weakened, while points with Δr = (n + ½)λ are reinforced. Since Δr = 3λ, P is weakened, with amplitude |A₁ − A₂|. Applying the in-phase rule directly would incorrectly identify it as reinforced."),
  ])

T("doppler", "Motion and Interactions", "conceptual",
  "When a wave source and observer move toward each other, the observer receives a higher frequency than the source emits; when they move apart, the observer receives a lower frequency. This is the Doppler effect. "
  "It changes the received frequency, not the source's frequency. It occurs with all types of waves and can be used for speed measurement.",
  ["Describe the Doppler effect and determine whether the received frequency increases or decreases",
   "Explain the Doppler effect in terms of the number of complete waves received per unit time",
   "Give examples of applications of the Doppler effect"],
  [("S1.2.6", "exact")],
  pre=[("wave_params", "hard"), ("J:sound_feature", "soft"), ("velocity", "soft")],
  qs=[
    C("basic", "When a sound source and an observer move toward each other, how does the frequency received by the observer compare with the source frequency?",
      "It is higher than the source frequency", ["It is lower than the source frequency", "It is the same as the source frequency", "It is first higher and then lower than the source frequency"],
      "When they approach one another, the observer receives more complete waves per unit time, so the received frequency and pitch increase. The received frequency is lower than the source frequency only when they move apart."),
    C("basic", "An ambulance with a constant horn frequency passes a pedestrian and then moves away. The pedestrian hears the pitch drop. Why?",
      "The pedestrian receives fewer complete waves per unit time", ["The ambulance's emitted frequency decreases", "The speed of sound in air decreases", "The Doppler effect shortens the sound's wavelength"],
      "As the ambulance moves away, the time between adjacent sound waves reaching the pedestrian increases, so the received frequency decreases. The source frequency and the speed of sound in air remain unchanged. The Doppler effect changes the received frequency, not the source frequency."),
    N("standard", "A stationary source emits sound at 400 Hz, and sound travels at 340 m/s. An observer moves toward the source at 34 m/s. How many complete waves per second does the observer receive (the received frequency), in Hz? (Enter a number only)",
      "440", "The wavelength is λ = v/f = 340 ÷ 400 = 0.85 m. The observer's speed relative to the waves is 340 + 34 = 374 m/s, so the received frequency is 374 ÷ 0.85 = 440 Hz, higher than 400 Hz.",
      "(340+34)*400/340"),
    C("standard", "Which application uses the Doppler effect?",
      "A traffic officer sends ultrasound toward a moving car and measures its speed from the frequency shift of the reflected waves",
      ["Measuring the depth of the seabed from an ultrasonic echo", "Using resonance to amplify a musical instrument's sound", "Hearing someone speak through a wall"],
      "The frequency shift of the reflected waves is related to the car's speed, which is how the officer measures it; this is the Doppler effect. Measuring sea depth uses echoes (reflection) and travel time, a resonator uses resonance, and hearing through a wall mainly involves diffraction, reflection, and transmission."),
  ])

# ---------------- Chapter 4: Light
T("refraction", "Motion and Interactions", "procedural",
  "When light enters another medium obliquely, its direction changes. The ratio of the sine of the angle of incidence i to the sine of the angle of refraction r is constant for a given medium and is called the refractive index: n = sin i / sin r. "
  "The refractive index also equals the speed of light in a vacuum divided by its speed in the medium, n = c/v. When light enters a medium, its frequency stays the same and its wavelength becomes 1/n of its vacuum wavelength.",
  ["Calculate with the law of refraction, n = sin i / sin r, and know that i and r are measured from the normal",
   "Find the speed of light in a medium using n = c/v and know that refractive indices are greater than 1",
   "Explain that light's frequency stays the same and its wavelength decreases when it enters a medium"],
  [("S1.3.1", "exact")],
  pre=[("J:refract", "hard"), ("J:reflect", "soft"), ("wave_params", "soft")],
  math=[("cnm_277", "The law of refraction uses sine values for special angles such as 30° and 60° to calculate refractive index.")],
  qs=[
    C("basic", "In the definition n = sin i / sin r, what do i and r represent?",
      "The angles between the incident and refracted rays, respectively, and the normal", ["The angles between the incident and refracted rays, respectively, and the surface", "The angle between the incident ray and the surface, and the angle between the refracted ray and the normal", "The angles of incidence and reflection"],
      "The angles in the law of refraction are measured between each ray and the normal, not the surface. If an angle is given relative to the surface, first convert it to its complement."),
    C("basic", "The speed of light in a vacuum is 3×10⁸ m/s, and the refractive index of glass is 1.5. What is the speed of light in glass?",
      "2×10⁸ m/s", ["4.5×10⁸ m/s", "3×10⁸ m/s", "1.5×10⁸ m/s"],
      "v = c/n = 3×10⁸ ÷ 1.5 = 2×10⁸ m/s. The refractive index is greater than 1, so light in a medium is always slower than c. Using v = nc gives 4.5×10⁸ m/s, greater than the speed of light in a vacuum, which is unreasonable."),
    N("standard", "Light enters a medium obliquely from air. The angle between the incident ray and the surface is 30°, and the angle between the refracted ray and the normal is 30°. What is the medium's refractive index? (sin 60° = √3/2; round to two decimal places; enter a number only)",
      "1.73", "The angle of incidence is i = 90° − 30° = 60° (between the incident ray and the normal), and the angle of refraction is r = 30°. Thus n = sin 60° / sin 30° = (√3/2) ÷ 0.5 = √3 ≈ 1.73. Treating 30° as the angle of incidence gives n = 1, contradicting the fact that refraction occurred.", "round(sqrt(3),2)"),
    C("advanced", "Monochromatic light with a fixed frequency has a wavelength of 600 nm in air. What is its wavelength in glass with a refractive index of 1.5?",
      "400 nm", ["900 nm", "600 nm", "267 nm"],
      "When light enters a medium, its frequency stays the same and its speed becomes c/n. From λ = v/f, λ' = λ/n = 600 ÷ 1.5 = 400 nm. Multiplying by n gives 900 nm; assuming wavelength is unchanged gives 600 nm; dividing by n² gives 267 nm. All are incorrect.",
      verify="600/1.5"),
  ])

T("exp_refraction", "Motion and Interactions", "procedural",
  "Measure the refractive index of a glass block using pins: place P₁ and P₂ on one side to define the incident ray; place P₃ and P₄ on the other side so they align with the images of P₁ and P₂, defining the emerging ray. Draw the refracted ray, measure the angles of incidence and refraction, and calculate n = sin i / sin r. The circle-construction method can also be used.",
  ["Explain the key steps and precautions in the pin method",
   "Find refractive index by construction, drawing a circle and measuring a ratio of line segments",
   "Analyze errors caused by misdrawing a glass-block boundary and similar mistakes"],
  [("S1.X.3", "exact"), ("S1.3.1", "inferred")],
  pre=[("refraction", "hard"), ("J:light_line", "soft")],
  qs=[
    C("basic", "When using the pin method to measure a glass block's refractive index, how should P₃ and P₄ be placed to determine the emerging ray?",
      "P₃ blocks the images of P₁ and P₂, and P₄ blocks P₃ and the images of P₁ and P₂", ["P₃ only needs to block P₁, and P₄ only needs to block P₂", "P₃ blocks the images of P₁ and P₂, and P₄ only needs to block the image of P₁", "Place P₃ and P₄ close to the glass block and align them with P₂"],
      "P₃ blocks the images of P₁ and P₂, and P₄ blocks P₃ and both images. This places P₃, P₄, and the images of P₁ and P₂ on the same ray; joining P₃ and P₄ gives the emerging ray. Aligning only some of the pins does not ensure they lie on the same light path."),
    C("basic", "Which procedure is appropriate when measuring refractive index with the pin method?",
      "Keep a reasonable distance between pins P₁ and P₂ and between P₃ and P₄",
      ["Make the angle of incidence as large as possible to make refraction obvious", "Touch the optical surface of the glass block to mark its boundary", "After drawing aa′, remove the glass block and use its edge as a ruler to draw bb′"],
      "A larger separation between pins reduces error in determining the ray direction. If the angle of incidence is too large, the emerging ray is dimmer and measurement error increases. Do not touch the optical surface or use the block as a ruler to draw the boundary; this can damage the surface and make bb′ inaccurate."),
    N("standard", "To measure refractive index by construction, draw a circle of radius R = 4.0 cm centered at point O of incidence. The circle meets the incident ray at A and the refracted ray at B. Draw perpendiculars from A and B to the normal; their lengths are 3.6 cm and 2.4 cm, respectively. What is the refractive index of the glass? (Enter a number only)",
      "1.5", "sin i = 3.6/4.0 = 0.9, sin r = 2.4/4.0 = 0.6, and n = sin i / sin r = 0.9 ÷ 0.6 = 1.5. The radius R cancels from the ratio, so n = (distance from A to the normal)/(distance from B to the normal) = 3.6/2.4 = 1.5. A and B must lie on the same circle centered at the incidence point. Treating 3.6 and 2.4 directly as sine values is incorrect because they exceed 1; divide by R first.", "3.6/2.4"),
    C("advanced", "When measuring a glass block's refractive index with the pin method, aa′ is drawn correctly, but bb′ is drawn farther out than the block's actual lower boundary, making the drawn block too wide. All other steps are correct. How does the measured refractive index compare with the true value?",
      "It is too small", ["It is too large", "It is unchanged", "It cannot be determined"],
      "P₃ and P₄ determine the emerging ray, which intersects the incorrectly drawn bb′ at O″. O″ lies farther out and to the side of the true exit point O′, so the drawn refracted ray OO″ makes a larger angle with the normal. The measured r is therefore too large, and n = sin i / sin r is too small."),
  ])

T("total_reflection", "Motion and Interactions", "reasoning",
  "When light travels from an optically denser to a less dense medium, the angle of refraction is greater than the angle of incidence. As the angle of incidence increases to the critical angle C, the refracted ray disappears and only the reflected ray remains; this is total internal reflection, with sin C = 1/n. "
  "Total internal reflection occurs when light travels from an optically denser to a less dense medium and the angle of incidence is at least the critical angle. Optical fibers use this effect.",
  ["State the conditions for total internal reflection and determine whether it can occur",
   "Use sin C = 1/n to find the critical angle and solve problems involving underwater light sources",
   "Know the structure of an optical fiber (the core has a higher refractive index than the cladding) and how it works"],
  [("S1.3.2", "exact")],
  pre=[("refraction", "hard")],
  math=[("cnm_278", "To find the radius of the illuminated region on the water's surface, use tan C and the side lengths of a right triangle.")],
  qs=[
    C("basic", "Under which conditions does total internal reflection occur?",
      "Light travels from an optically denser to a less dense medium, and the angle of incidence is at least the critical angle", ["Light travels from an optically less dense to a denser medium at a sufficiently large angle of incidence", "Light travels from an optically denser to a less dense medium at an angle smaller than the critical angle", "It occurs whenever light leaves any medium at an angle greater than 45°"],
      "Total internal reflection requires both conditions: light travels from an optically denser to a less dense medium, and the angle of incidence is at least the critical angle. When light enters a denser medium from a less dense one, the angle of refraction is always smaller than the angle of incidence, so total internal reflection cannot occur. The critical angle depends on the refractive index; it is not always 45°."),
    C("basic", "Light travels from a medium with a refractive index of 2 into air. What is the critical angle for total internal reflection?",
      "30°", ["60°", "45°", "90°"],
      "sin C = 1/n = 1/2, so C = 30°. Mistaking sin C for cos C gives 60°. Total internal reflection occurs when the angle of incidence is at least 30°.",
      verify="30"),
    N("standard", "A point source of light is 1.6 m below the surface of a liquid with a refractive index of 5/3. The light emerging from the surface covers a circular area. What is the circle's radius in meters? (sin 37° = 0.6, cos 37° = 0.8; enter a number only)",
      "1.2", "sin C = 1/n = 0.6, so C = 37°. The ray that reaches the surface at an angle of incidence C is just at the threshold for total internal reflection. The radius is R = h·tan C = 1.6 × (0.6/0.8) = 1.2 m. Using R = h·sin C gives 0.96 m.", "1.6*0.6/0.8"),
    C("standard", "An optical fiber has a core and cladding, and light travels through it by total internal reflection. How should their refractive indices compare?",
      "The core's refractive index is greater than the cladding's", ["The core's refractive index is less than the cladding's", "The core and cladding have the same refractive index", "It is enough for both to be greater than 1; their relative values do not matter"],
      "For total internal reflection at the core-cladding boundary, light must travel from the optically denser core into the less dense cladding, so the core's refractive index must be greater."),
  ])

T("light_interference", "Motion and Interactions", "reasoning",
  "Light passing through a double slit produces two coherent beams that overlap on a screen, forming alternating bright and dark fringes. Bright fringes occur where the path difference is an integer multiple of the wavelength; dark fringes occur where it is an odd multiple of half the wavelength. The spacing between adjacent fringes is Δx = lλ/d. "
  "Fringe spacing is proportional to wavelength and inversely proportional to slit separation; white light produces colored interference fringes.",
  ["Explain the principle of double-slit interference and the conditions for bright and dark fringes",
   "Calculate fringe spacing with Δx = lλ/d and analyze how different factors affect it",
   "Explain how interference demonstrates the wave nature of light"],
  [("S1.3.3", "exact")],
  pre=[("wave_interference", "hard"), ("refraction", "soft"), ("J:dispersion", "soft")],
  math=[("cnm_p214", "Fringe spacing is inversely proportional to slit separation d; use inverse proportion to analyze how Δx changes with d.")],
  qs=[
    C("basic", "In Young's double-slit experiment, why are two slits placed after the light source?",
      "To split one beam into two waves with the same frequency and a constant phase difference", ["To increase the light intensity and make the fringes brighter", "To refract the light and change its direction", "To make the light monochromatic by removing all other colors"],
      "Light from the same source passes through the two slits to produce waves with the same frequency and a constant phase difference, enabling stable interference. The slits do not intensify the light; a filter is used to obtain monochromatic light."),
    C("basic", "In a double-slit experiment, monochromatic light has a wavelength of 600 nm. The path difference between the two slits and point P on the screen is 0.9 μm. What appears at P?",
      "A dark fringe, where the waves weaken one another", ["A bright fringe, where the waves reinforce one another", "A fringe of intermediate brightness", "A dark fringe because no light reaches the point"],
      "0.9 μm = 900 nm = 1.5λ = (n + ½)λ (n = 1), so the path difference is an odd multiple of half the wavelength and the waves weaken one another, producing a dark fringe. A dark fringe does not mean no light reaches the point; it results from cancellation when the waves overlap."),
    N("standard", "In a double-slit experiment, the slit separation is d = 0.30 mm, the distance from the slits to the screen is l = 1.5 m, and the monochromatic light has a wavelength of 600 nm. What is the spacing in mm between adjacent bright-fringe centers? (Enter a number only)",
      "3", "Δx = lλ/d = 1.5 × 600×10⁻⁹ ÷ (0.30×10⁻³) = 3×10⁻³ m = 3 mm. Convert all units to meters before calculating; forgetting to convert nanometers or millimeters causes errors of several orders of magnitude.", "round(1.5*600e-9/0.30e-3*1000,6)"),
    C("standard", "With all other conditions unchanged, which action increases the spacing between adjacent bright fringes on the screen?",
      "Use monochromatic light with a longer wavelength", ["Increase the slit separation", "Reduce the distance from the slits to the screen", "Use monochromatic light with a higher frequency"],
      "Δx = lλ/d: fringe spacing is proportional to wavelength λ and distance l from the slits to the screen, and inversely proportional to slit separation d. Increasing d, reducing l, or using higher-frequency (shorter-wavelength) light all decrease Δx."),
  ])

T("exp_double_slit", "Motion and Interactions", "procedural",
  "Measure the wavelength of light using double-slit interference: arrange the source, filter, single slit, double slit, light shield, and screen in order. Measure the distance between the centers of n bright fringes to find Δx = (aₙ − a₁)/(n − 1), then calculate wavelength using λ = Δx·d/l. "
  "When processing data, use consistent units for mm, m, and nm, and distinguish the number of fringes from the number of gaps between them.",
  ["State the order of the equipment and the function of each component",
   "Find Δx from measurement-head readings and calculate wavelength with λ = Δx·d/l",
   "Analyze errors such as confusing the number of fringes with the number of intervals"],
  [("S1.X.4", "exact"), ("S1.3.3", "inferred")],
  pre=[("light_interference", "hard"), ("exp_length", "soft"), ("J:dispersion", "soft")],
  qs=[
    C("basic", "In an apparatus for measuring wavelength with double-slit interference, what is the correct order of the components on the optical bench, starting from the light source?",
      "Light source, filter, single slit, double slit, light shield, screen", ["Light source, double slit, single slit, filter, light shield, screen", "Light source, single slit, filter, double slit, light shield, screen", "Light source, filter, double slit, single slit, light shield, screen"],
      "Light from the source first passes through a filter to become monochromatic, then through a single slit to form a line source, and then through a double slit to produce two coherent waves. They travel through the light shield and form interference fringes on the screen. The single slit must come before the double slit."),
    N("basic", "The slit separation is d = 0.20 mm, the distance from the slits to the screen is l = 0.80 m, and the spacing between adjacent bright-fringe centers is Δx = 2.4 mm. What is the wavelength in nm? (Enter a number only)",
      "600", "λ = Δx·d/l = 2.4×10⁻³ × 0.20×10⁻³ ÷ 0.80 = 6.0×10⁻⁷ m = 600 nm. Inconsistent units, such as treating mm as m, are a common mistake.", "round(2.4e-3*0.20e-3/0.80*1e9,3)"),
    N("standard", "A measuring head reads a₁ = 0.50 mm at the center of the first bright fringe and a₆ = 8.00 mm at the center of the sixth. The slit separation is d = 0.30 mm and the screen is l = 0.90 m from the slits. What is the wavelength in nm? (Enter a number only)",
      "500", "There are 5 intervals between the first and sixth bright fringes, so Δx = (a₆ − a₁)/5 = 7.50 mm ÷ 5 = 1.5 mm. λ = Δx·d/l = 1.5×10⁻³ × 0.30×10⁻³ ÷ 0.90 = 5×10⁻⁷ m = 500 nm. Dividing by 6 instead would give about 417 nm.", "round((8.00-0.50)/5*1e-3*0.30e-3/0.90*1e9,3)"),
    C("standard", "A measuring head gives a distance a between the centers of the first and nth bright fringes. The experimenter incorrectly calculates Δx as a/n instead of the correct a/(n − 1); all other data are accurate. How does the measured wavelength compare with the true value?",
      "It is too small", ["It is too large", "It is equal", "It cannot be determined"],
      "There are only n − 1 intervals between n bright fringes, so Δx should be a/(n − 1). Using a/n makes Δx too small, and λ = Δx·d/l means the measured wavelength is too small. For example, a₆ − a₁ spans 5 intervals, not 6."),
  ])

T("diffraction", "Motion and Interactions", "conceptual",
  "The bending of light around obstacles is called diffraction. Diffraction is noticeable when an opening or obstacle is about the same size as or smaller than the wavelength of light. "
  "In single-slit diffraction, the central bright fringe is the widest and brightest, while the side fringes are narrower and dimmer. The Poisson bright spot at the center of a disk's shadow is strong evidence for the wave nature of light.",
  ["State the conditions for noticeable diffraction of light",
   "Distinguish single-slit diffraction fringes from double-slit interference fringes",
   "Explain how the Poisson spot and similar phenomena show that light has wave properties"],
  [("S1.3.3", "exact")],
  pre=[("wave_boundary", "hard"), ("light_interference", "soft"), ("J:light_line", "soft")],
  qs=[
    C("basic", "Under what condition does light diffract noticeably?",
      "When the size of an obstacle or opening is about the same as or smaller than the wavelength of light", ["When the obstacle or opening is much larger than the wavelength of light", "When the light has a very low frequency", "Only when the light is white"],
      "All waves can diffract, but diffraction is noticeable only when an obstacle or opening is about the same size as or smaller than the wavelength. If it is much larger, light travels approximately in a straight line."),
    C("basic", "Monochromatic light passes through a slit and forms diffraction fringes on a screen. How do they differ from double-slit interference fringes?",
      "The diffraction fringes have unequal widths, and the central bright fringe is the widest and brightest", ["The diffraction fringes are equally spaced and equally bright", "The central diffraction fringe is the narrowest, and the side fringes get wider", "Diffraction fringes appear only when the slit is much wider than the wavelength"],
      "The central single-slit diffraction fringe is the widest and brightest; the side fringes are narrower and quickly become dimmer. Double-slit interference fringes are nearly equally spaced and similarly bright. Diffraction is not noticeable when the slit is much wider than the wavelength."),
    C("standard", "An opaque disk is illuminated by a point source, and a bright spot (the Poisson spot) appears at the center of its shadow. What does this show?",
      "Light has wave properties and has diffracted", ["Light travels in a straight line", "Light is a transverse wave", "Light has undergone total internal reflection"],
      "If light traveled only in straight lines, the center of the disk's shadow would be dark. The bright spot results from light diffracting around the disk's edge and overlapping, showing that light has wave properties. Polarization, not the Poisson spot, demonstrates that light is transverse."),
    C("standard", "Monochromatic light passes through a single slit and forms diffraction fringes on a screen. The slit is always wider than the wavelength. As the slit gets narrower, how do the width and brightness of the central bright fringe change?",
      "It gets wider and dimmer", ["It gets narrower and brighter", "It gets wider and brighter", "It gets narrower and dimmer"],
      "A narrower slit makes diffraction more noticeable and widens the central bright fringe. Less light passes through, so the fringes become dimmer. Assuming the fringes narrow with the slit confuses diffraction with rectilinear propagation."),
  ])

T("polarization", "Motion and Interactions", "conceptual",
  "In unpolarized light, vibration intensity is equal in all directions perpendicular to propagation; polarized light vibrates in only one specific direction. The fact that light can be polarized shows that it is a transverse wave. "
  "Two polarizing filters transmit the most light when their transmission axes are parallel and almost none when they are perpendicular. Polarization is used in photography, 3D movies, and other applications.",
  ["Explain polarization and how it shows that light is a transverse wave",
   "Determine how transmitted intensity changes as the axes of two polarizing filters rotate relative to one another",
   "Give examples of applications of polarization"],
  [("S1.3.3", "exact")],
  pre=[("wave_formation", "hard"), ("diffraction", "soft"), ("J:reflect", "soft")],
  qs=[
    C("basic", "What does the ability of light to be polarized show about light?",
      "It is a transverse wave", ["It is a longitudinal wave", "It is a stream of particles", "It is neither a transverse nor a longitudinal wave"],
      "Only transverse waves can be polarized: their vibrations are perpendicular to their direction of propagation, so vibrations can be restricted to one direction. Light can be polarized, showing that it is a transverse wave."),
    C("basic", "Unpolarized light passes through polarizer P and then polarizer Q. Q is slowly rotated once around the direction of propagation. How does the transmitted intensity change?",
      "It alternates between maximum twice and almost zero twice", ["It stays constant", "It reaches a maximum once and almost zero once", "It steadily increases"],
      "Light transmission is strongest when Q's transmission axis is parallel to P's (0° and 180°) and nearly zero when the axes are perpendicular (90° and 270°), giving two maxima and two minima per rotation. If light were longitudinal, rotating the polarizer would not produce this change."),
    C("standard", "Why add a polarizing filter in front of a camera lens when photographing objects underwater?",
      "To reduce reflected light from the water's surface and make underwater objects clearer", ["To increase surface reflections and brighten the image", "To change the color of underwater objects", "To convert reflected light from the surface into unpolarized light"],
      "Reflected light from the water's surface is polarized. Adjusting the filter's transmission axis blocks most of it while having less effect on light from underwater objects, making the image clearer. A polarizer does not change object colors or turn polarized light back into unpolarized light."),
    C("standard", "Which of the following waves cannot be polarized?",
      "Sound traveling through air", ["Visible light", "Radio waves", "A transverse wave traveling along a rope through a slit"],
      "Polarization is characteristic of transverse waves. Sound in air is longitudinal, with particles vibrating along the direction of propagation, so it cannot be polarized. Light and radio waves are transverse electromagnetic waves, and a transverse wave on a rope is blocked by a slit perpendicular to its vibration direction."),
  ])

T("laser", "Motion and Interactions", "application",
  "Laser light is artificially produced and has high coherence, strong directionality, and high intensity. "
  "Its coherence is used in holography and as a carrier in fiber-optic communications; its directionality enables precise ranging; and its high intensity (concentrated energy) is used for cutting, welding, and surgery.",
  ["State the characteristics of laser light",
   "Match laser applications to the corresponding characteristics",
   "Perform simple calculations involving laser ranging and power"],
  [("S1.3.4", "exact")],
  pre=[("light_interference", "hard"), ("J:light_line", "soft"), ("em_wave", "soft")],
  qs=[
    C("basic", "Compared with light from ordinary sources, what are the main characteristics of laser light?",
      "High coherence, strong directionality, and high intensity", ["Low coherence, strong directionality, and high intensity", "High coherence, weak directionality, and low intensity", "High coherence, strong directionality, and many different frequencies"],
      "Laser light has high coherence (a narrow frequency range and a stable phase relationship), strong directionality (it barely diverges), and high intensity (its energy is highly concentrated). It is highly monochromatic and does not contain a wide range of frequencies."),
    C("basic", "Which laser application mainly uses its high coherence?",
      "Holography", ["Laser cutting of metal", "Measuring the distance from Earth to the Moon with a laser", "Laser surgery"],
      "Holography uses coherent light to record information about an object through interference. Cutting and surgery use high intensity and concentrated energy; ranging uses strong directionality."),
    N("standard", "A laser pulse is sent toward the Moon. The round-trip time until the reflected pulse returns is 2.56 s. If the speed of light is 3.0×10⁸ m/s, about how far is the Moon from Earth in km? (Enter a number only)",
      "384000", "The light travels a round-trip distance, so the one-way distance is s = ct/2 = 3.0×10⁸ × 2.56 ÷ 2 = 3.84×10⁸ m = 384,000 km. Forgetting to divide by 2 gives 768,000 km; failing to convert meters to kilometers gives an answer 1,000 times too large.", "round(3.0e8*2.56/2/1000,3)"),
    N("standard", "A pulsed laser emits a pulse with an energy of 2 J and a duration of 1×10⁻⁸ s. What is the pulse's average power in MW? (Enter a number only)",
      "200", "P = W/t = 2 J ÷ (1×10⁻⁸ s) = 2×10⁸ W = 200 MW. A laser concentrates energy into a very short time and small area, producing high power for uses such as cutting and welding.", "round(2/1e-8/1e6,3)"),
  ])
